  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\n\nThe goal is to minimize the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap constant C. We tackle this by leveraging a continuous optimization strategy over a higher-resolution step function representation. The method involves:\n1. An interpolating projection from the initial 250-point parent solution to a user-defined resolution\n2. A gradient-based optimization with analytical gradient computation for the max-shift overlap integral\n3. Multiple resolution scalings (from 500 to 8000 points) with progressive complexity\n4. Adaptive optimization based on gradient signal-to-noise ratio in problematic regions\n\nThe core insight is that continuous optimization on higher resolutions can escape local minima of step function structures. We compute the worst-case shift at each optimization iteration and derive its gradient by finite differences between shifted configurations. This ensures we directly optimize the critical shift direction, unlike previous approximations.\n\nThe mathematical formulation is:\n\nMinimize: max_k [\u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx]\nSubject to: \u2211h_i * dx = 1 (with dx=2/n), 0\u2264h_i\u22641\n\nThe gradient is computed as:\n\n\u25bdC = dx[ \n  (1 - h(i+k))   -   h(i-k) \n  for worst shift k per optimization step ]\n\nMathematical specification\n\nWe represent the step function h on a grid of size n as a vector of real numbers between 0 and 1. The optimization domain is defined by:\n\n- Grid length n = {500,750,1000,1500,2000,2500,3000,4000,5000,8000}\n- Window constraints: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n- Integral constraint: sum(h) = n/2 (exact)\n- Objective: C5 = max_lag [correlation(h,1-h][lag] * dx]\n\nImplementation procedure\n\n1. Parent interpolation:\n   - Input parameters: n (grid size), seed for restarts\n   - Interpolate initial_h_values to desired resolution (e.g., log_spacing)\n   - Maintain exact integral by: total = n/2, current_total = interpolated_sum, scale_factor = total/current_total \u2192 h_new = interpolated * scale_factor\n\n2. Solver configuration:\n   - Use SLSQP optimizer from scipy.optimize\n   - Bounds: [(0,1)] for all grid points\n   - Linear constraint: [1]*n input \u2192 sum(x) = n/2\n\n3. Gradient computation:\n   - Base implementation: brute-force O(n\u00b2) correlation\n   - Alternative: FFT-based O(n log n) if feasible with custom implementation\n   - Must track the worst (largest) shift lag k0 = argmax_lag\n   - Gradient: h[i] update \u2192 dx[(1 - h[i+k0]) - h[i-k0]]\n\n4. Optimization phases:\n   Phase 1 (default): Basic gradient descent\n   Phase 2: Restart system with geometric coordinate scaling (0.8, 0.9, 0.99) for suspicious minima\n   Phase 3: Multi-resolution hierarchy (from 500 to 8000 points)\n   Phase 4: Warm-start integration for progressive complexity\n\nSearch choices and useful variations\n\n1. Resolution: Default: 2500 (progressive 500,1000,1500,...)\n2. Optimization strategy:\n   - Self-adaptive gradient accumulation: Track gradient relative to objective\n   - Stochastic selection of grid points for random projection restarts\n3. Initializations:\n   - Weighted parent projection\n   - Normal perturbation: h[i] + randn[i] * std_parent\n4. Varations:\n   - Adaptive resolution switching based on gradient noise floor\n   - Penalized oscillation detection: if gradient magnitude plateaus, force coordinate scaling\n   - Temperature schedule: gradually increase constraint violation tolerance\n\nComputebudget and robustness\n\nThe dominant cost is the correlation computation (n\u00b2 operations per evaluation).\nThe budget is split across:\n- 20%: Gradient evaluation\n- 80%: Optimization iterations\n\nEstimate:\n\n   n=500: correlation takes ~250ms (assumed)\n   n=8000: (if enabled) takes ~64 million times longer \u2192 about 30min\n\nWe allocate time per resolution segment and enforce:\n$ \\text{total_time} \\ge 1100\\text{s}$\n$ \\tau_i = \\max(\\tau_{i-1} + 20\\%, 10.\\text{s}\\ minimum) $ for new resolution\n\nValidation and return contract\n1. Ensure [0,1] via projection at each step\n2. Verify sum(h)*(2/n) == target (allow \u00b1ATOL=1e-3)\n3. Re-evaluate objective via full correlation for final candidate\n\nReturns: (h_values, best_c5, n_points)\n\nArrays must be provided in [0,1] and satisfy the integral constraint exactly via projection if necessary.\n\nThis plan utilizes continuous optimization on progressively refined grids to escape local optima, unlike previous binary representations. The adaptive gradient computation and multi-resolution strategy address the main bottlenecks of prior approaches: plateauing at local minima and insufficient resolution.\n\nThe fallback solution is:\n\n   h = initial_h_values interpolated to n=2000 points\n\nValidation parameter checks include overflow checks and early termination for stability.",
